{
  peppy_schema: "contract/v1",
  manifest: {
    name: "simulation_world",
    tag: "v1",
    description: [
      "The worlds of a simulation: the record of its setup, the build of a world in one step, the expansion of a world pattern into a world, the measure of the work cell of the world, and the judges that check ground truth in it. It is for the applier of worlds, which rebuilds the world of a past conversation and runs the sweeps of a workflow, and for an evaluation that scores a built world; no model of a chat calls it.",
      "The setup record is the last value of each setup call the simulation took (scene_manipulation, object_controls, scene_lighting, scene_materials, scene_daylight and scene_workspace): the scene and its scale, the scene objects moved, removed or with a control set, the objects spawned, the lighting, the materials, the daylight, the regions of the world that build built, the named locations and the stand of each robot with a body. It holds call values, never physics: the clock, apply_force, looks, listings, robot motions and camera settings do not change it, and the daylight's hour is the one set_daylight_time set, whatever the daylight's clock ran since. A world document (world/v1) is that record with the join of each robot, which the stack listing gives.",
      "The apply id names the world that is live. It changes at each start_apply and at each start of the simulation, so two equal apply ids name one apply of one world, and a client that holds an apply id knows whether the world it applied is still the one that stands.",
      "The setup claim (claim) gives the setup to one client at a time. While it lasts, only the holder makes setup calls: each call of scene_manipulation, object_controls, scene_lighting, scene_materials, scene_daylight and scene_workspace that changes the setup record, apply_force and move_robot, set_paused and step of simulation_clock, and start_apply and build here. The simulation refuses those of every other client with `precondition_failed`, naming the claim and its holder. A spawn by a feeder goes through, and so do reads, views, camera settings and sensor holds. The pause of the viewer and the hand teleoperation of the simulation's page carry no client: while the claim lasts, the simulation refuses each pause and each daylight edit from the viewer, and each drive of the hand teleoperation on a robot that the holder has not named with allow_hand_teleop. It stands a joining robot (attach of simulation_robot) only when the last start_apply of the claim named it, and refuses each other attach with `precondition_failed`, naming the claim; a robot that leaves goes through, and so does a standing robot that registers again.",
      "A world pattern is a world/v1 with samplers in place of some values: one_of and range in any value but a join, on (a surface, with reachable_by) and in (a region of the world) in an object pose, in in the position of a stand, entries of an item kind with a count, work_cell_parameters and min_spacing_m. A seed picks one value from each sampler (expand). Each sampler draws from its own stream, which the seed and the path of the sampler in the drawn world give, so the same pattern, seed, catalogue, robot models and simulation give the same world, and an added entry changes no other value. A work cell parameter is a number that an operator can measure in a real work cell: a control value of an object, or the distance from the base of a robot to a surface label or a landmark (measure).",
      "Members carry documents as JSON5 text: the setup record, a world/v1 and a world pattern, the list of judges and the list of verdicts, the work cell parameters and their measurements, each in the form the simulation documents of the simulation side define.",
      "A simulation that lets no caller edit or inspect its world answers each member with `rejected`: an error reply to a service, a refusal to the build.",
    ],
  },
  failure_kinds: {
    seed_refused: "The seed gives no world of the world pattern: a sampler finds no value, a name that an entry of an item kind adds is a scene object name of the drawn scene, or the simulation refuses the drawn world; the message names the entry",
  },
  interfaces: {
    services: [
      {
        name: "get",
        description: "Give the setup record and the apply id of the live world, and the catalogue release the simulation runs: the digest of the release, or \"local\" for a simulation with a local catalogue directory, whose world documents say \"local\" too. A simulation that cannot write its setup record as JSON5 gives an error reply with `attempt_failed`.",
        request_message_format: {
          start: { $type: "bool", $description: "True for the setup the simulation started with: the opening scene, nothing set up, and the stand of each robot with a body where it first joined. False for the live setup." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          setup_record: { $type: "string", $description: "The setup record, as JSON5 text." },
          first_stands: { $type: "string", $description: "With start, the robots with a body that joined the simulation, each where it first joined: a JSON5 object from robot name to { position, yaw }. Otherwise an empty object." },
          apply_id: "string",
          catalogue: "string",
        },
      },
      {
        name: "start_apply",
        description: "Announce an apply and the robots of its world: the applier of worlds calls it, and the answer gives the new apply id. The apply id changes, so a failed apply never keeps the old one. While a claim lasts, only the holder calls it, and its robots are the robots that a join may stand until the next start_apply or the end of the claim; another sender gets `precondition_failed`, naming the claim.",
        request_message_format: {
          robots: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The names of the robots of the world, as the stack names them." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          apply_id: "string",
        },
      },
      {
        name: "judge",
        description: "Give the verdicts of judges in the live world: for each judge, whether it holds and why, in the order of the judges. A list that does not parse, and a judge that names an object, a landmark, a region, a location or a control the world does not hold, or an item kind that no asset of the catalogue carries, get an error reply with `rejected` that names it, as does a reachable judge whose robot or target the simulation cannot measure (a robot model whose arms it cannot measure, an object with no bounding box), and a judge that names a robot that does not stand in the simulation one with `resource_unavailable`; no verdict is given. An item kind that assets of the catalogue carry and no standing object has counts 0. A simulation that cannot read its world gives an error reply with `precondition_failed`, and one that cannot write the verdicts as JSON5 one with `attempt_failed`.",
        request_message_format: {
          judges: { $type: "string", $description: "A JSON5 list of judges." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          verdicts: { $type: "string", $description: "A JSON5 list of { holds, why }, one for each judge." },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "measure",
        description: "Give the value of each work cell parameter in the live world: for a control, the value of the control of its object; for a distance, the coordinate along an axis of the frame of a robot base (ahead, its +X; side, its +Y, to its left; up, its +Z) of the centre of a landmark, or of the point of the top of a surface label nearest to the base, from the base of each robot of per_robot, or of the one robot with a stand without per_robot. Changes nothing. A list that does not parse, and a parameter that names an object, a control, a surface label or a landmark the world does not hold, get an error reply with `rejected` that names it, and a parameter that names a robot that does not stand in the simulation one with `resource_unavailable`; no value is given. A simulation that cannot read its world gives an error reply with `precondition_failed`, and one that cannot write the values as JSON5 one with `attempt_failed`.",
        request_message_format: {
          parameters: { $type: "string", $description: "The work cell parameters of a world pattern, as JSON5 text: an object from each name to { unit, control } or { unit, distance, per_robot }." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          values: { $type: "string", $description: "The value of each parameter, as JSON5 text: an object from each name to its value, or, for a parameter with per_robot, to an object from each robot to its value." },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "allow_hand_teleop",
        description: "Set the robots that the hand teleoperation may drive while the claim lasts; each call replaces the set. A claim starts with none, and the set ends with the claim; without a claim the hand teleoperation drives any robot. Refused with `precondition_failed` for a sender that does not hold the claim, naming the claim, and with `rejected` for a name that is not a robot name.",
        request_message_format: {
          robots: { $type: "array", $items: "string" },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          robots: { $type: "array", $items: "string" },
        },
      },
    ],
    actions: [
      {
        name: "build",
        description: "Build a concrete world in one step, with the clock paused: the scene at its scale, each scene object moved, removed or with a control set, each object at its pose with its controls, a height control before what stands on its object and a support before what it carries, the lighting, the materials and the daylight, which start from the values of the scene and take the world's own lighting and materials entries and its daylight on top, the daylight's clock running from the build, each camera from the defaults of its device, each robot with a body at its stand, the regions of the world kept, and each location named. The objects then settle for 2 s of simulated time, and the clock runs. A step of simulation_clock in progress ends cancelled, with a reason that names the build. Before any change, the build is refused, naming the field: with `rejected` for a world the simulation cannot build as written, which is a document that does not parse or extends a base, a catalogue release other than the simulation's, a scene that load_scene of scene_manipulation refuses at the world's scale, an object that spawn_object refuses (an asset the catalogue does not hold, and a pose, a scale, a physics or a mass the simulation does not take, an object without a mass when the catalogue gives its asset none among them), an object that names no physics (the catalogue gives no asset one), a scene object that is not a placement of the scene, a landmark that the asset of its object does not declare, a location of an object the world does not hold, a stand or a location at a surface the scene does not declare or whose object the world removes, a named pose the catalogue does not hold, and a daylight that scene_daylight refuses (a date, an hour, a rate or a weather its setters refuse, or any daylight for a scene that has none); and with `resource_unavailable` for a robot that does not stand in the simulation. A refused build changes nothing, and so does a build that fails with `attempt_failed` because the files of the world do not come. A build fails after its first change with `precondition_failed` when the world as it stands refuses a step, as when the implementer's placement rules refuse a placement or another caller runs or steps the clock, and with `attempt_failed` for any other cause the simulation cannot build, such as a control that its object lacks or a light or a material that the scene lacks; get then reports the setup record as the steps left it. A failed build puts the clock back as it was before the build, and each robot it moved but did not stand back where it stood; a robot it cannot put back stays where the build left it, and the failure names it. A build ends each load_scene and spawn_object of scene_manipulation admitted before it that has not handed its command to the simulation, cancelled with a reason that names the build. It takes the place of a load: a load_scene or a later build that arrives while it fetches its files or waits for an earlier load to be built ends it cancelled, with a reason that names what replaced it. Once it builds, it holds back every load and spawn until it is built. The feedback is that of load_scene of scene_manipulation, over the files of the scene and the objects. While a claim lasts, a sender other than the holder is refused with `precondition_failed`, naming the claim. The result gives the setup record of the built world.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            world: { $type: "string", $description: "A concrete world/v1 document, as JSON5 text." },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            bytes_fetched: "u64",
            files_ready: "u64",
            building: "bool",
            bytes_ready: "u64",
            bytes_total: "u64",
            bytes_per_second: "u64",
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            setup_record: { $type: "string", $description: "The setup record of the built world, as JSON5 text; empty for a build that ends cancelled." },
          },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "expand",
        description: "Give the concrete world of a world pattern and a seed, drawn in a model of the drawn world, apart from the live world. The seed first draws the scene, the controls, each other one_of and range, and the count and the asset of each object of an item kind. The simulation then draws each stand and each pose on a surface or in a region after the values that it depends on, in the order that the simulation documents give: a pose with reachable_by where the robot reaches and sees it, by the rules of reach and view of find_places of scene_workspace, and each sampled object at least min_spacing_m from each other one. Changes neither the live world nor the setup record, and takes no claim. Before any draw, refused with `rejected` for a pattern that does not parse or extends a base, a catalogue release other than the simulation's, a scene that load_scene of scene_manipulation refuses, an item kind that no asset of the catalogue carries, a robot model that the simulation cannot measure, a surface or a landmark that a scene or an object of the pattern does not declare, a named pose the catalogue does not hold, and a cycle of stands and poses that depend on each other, each naming its entry. Fails with seed_refused for a seed that gives no world, naming the entry, and with `attempt_failed` when the files of the drawn world do not come. The feedback is that of build, over the files of the drawn world. The result gives the concrete world, named for the pattern and the seed.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            pattern: { $type: "string", $description: "A world pattern that extends no base, as JSON5 text." },
            seed: "u32",
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            bytes_fetched: "u64",
            files_ready: "u64",
            building: "bool",
            bytes_ready: "u64",
            bytes_total: "u64",
            bytes_per_second: "u64",
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            world: { $type: "string", $description: "The concrete world/v1 document, as JSON5 text, named <pattern>_seed_<seed> with the revision of the pattern." },
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["seed_refused"],
      },
      {
        name: "claim",
        description: "The setup claim, under a name that each refusal gives to other clients. Accepted when no client holds the claim, and when its sender holds it already, which hands the claim to this goal: the goal before ends cancelled, saying that a later claim of the same client holds it now. Refused with `precondition_failed`, naming the claim and its holder, and with `rejected` for an empty name. The feedback gives what the claim stands: once when the goal holds the claim, with no robot in either list, and again after each start_apply and each allow_hand_teleop of the holder. The goal never completes: the claim ends when its holder cancels it or is gone, and the goal ends cancelled for that reason.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            name: "string",
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            apply_robots: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The robots of the last start_apply of the claim: the ones a join stands." },
            hand_teleop_robots: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The robots of the last allow_hand_teleop of the claim: the ones the hand teleoperation drives." },
          },
        },
      },
    ],
  },
}
